<section id="intro">

<p class="eyebrow">Documentation</p>

# Build a visual filter, get back queryable code.

<div class="lede">

This library renders the rule tree your users edit, then converts that tree to SQL,
MongoDB, or any of 21 other formats. The whole thing is one Svelte component plus a few
helper functions. Everything on this page is a live demo. Click around, then copy the
code.

</div>

<Intro />

</section>

<section id="install">

## Install

The library ships as a single package. Run this in your project root.

```bash
pnpm add svelte-query-builder
```

<div class="muted small">

Internally it uses `@react-querybuilder/core` for format and parser logic. You do not need
to install that package yourself. The library expects Svelte 5 and works in SvelteKit or
any Vite based Svelte setup.

</div>

</section>

<section id="first">

## Your first builder

The component takes two things: the list of fields the user can pick from, and the query
they are editing. Use `bind:query` so changes inside the builder flow back to your variable.

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import { QueryBuilder, formatQuery } from 'svelte-query-builder';
  import type { Field, RuleGroupType } from 'svelte-query-builder';

  const fields: Field[] = [
    { name: 'firstName', label: 'First name' },
    { name: 'age', label: 'Age', inputType: 'number' },
  ];

  let query = $state<RuleGroupType>({ combinator: 'and', rules: [] });
</script>

<QueryBuilder bind:query {fields} />

<pre>{formatQuery(query, 'sql')}</pre>
```

Result: every change the user makes triggers a re-render of your `formatQuery` call, so
the SQL string stays in sync.

</section>

<section id="fields">

## Defining fields

Each entry in the `fields` array describes one column the user can filter on. The shape is
small. Most apps only ever set `name` and `label`. The rest is optional and lets you control
how the value editor renders for that field.

```ts
const fields: Field[] = [
  // Plain text
  { name: 'firstName', label: 'First name' },

  // Number
  { name: 'age', label: 'Age', inputType: 'number' },

  // Boolean checkbox
  { name: 'active', label: 'Active', valueEditorType: 'checkbox', defaultValue: false },

  // Select with fixed options
  {
    name: 'instrument',
    label: 'Instrument',
    valueEditorType: 'select',
    values: [
      { name: 'Guitar', label: 'Guitar' },
      { name: 'Piano',  label: 'Piano'  },
    ],
  },

  // Date that can be compared to another date field
  {
    name: 'startDate',
    label: 'Start',
    inputType: 'date',
    valueSources: ['value', 'field'],
  },

  // Array column with match modes (all / some / none / atLeast / atMost / exactly)
  { name: 'phones', label: 'Phone numbers', matchModes: true },
];
```

</section>

<section id="props">

## Component options

These are the props you pass to `<QueryBuilder>`. None are required apart from one of
`query`, `bind:query`, or `defaultQuery`.

| Prop | Type | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `query` | `RuleGroupType` | Controlled query value. Use with `onQueryChange` or `bind:query`. |
| `defaultQuery` | `RuleGroupType` | Initial query when running uncontrolled. Internal state takes over after that. |
| `onQueryChange` | `(q) => void` | Fires after every change made inside the builder. |
| `fields` | `Field[]` | The columns the user can pick from. |
| `independentCombinators` | `boolean` | Switches to the layout where AND or OR sits between rules instead of on the group. |
| `showNotToggle` | `boolean` | Adds a NOT toggle on each group. |
| `showCloneButtons` | `boolean` | Adds a button that duplicates a rule or group. |
| `showShiftActions` | `boolean` | Adds up and down arrows that move a rule within its group. |
| `showLockButtons` | `boolean` | Adds a lock toggle. Locked rules cannot be edited. |
| `showMuteButtons` | `boolean` | Adds a mute toggle. Muted rules stay in the tree but are skipped by `formatQuery`. |
| `enableDragAndDrop` | `boolean` | Lets the user reorder rules and groups by dragging them. |
| `validator` | `(q) => ValidationMap` | Decides which rules are valid. Invalid rules get a CSS class you can target. |
| `controls` | `Partial<Controls>` | Replace any built in subcomponent with your own. |
| `controlClassnames` | `Partial<Classnames>` | Add CSS classes to specific parts of the UI. |
| `translations` | `Partial<Translations>` | Override built in labels and titles. |

</section>

<section id="formats">

## Output formats

`formatQuery` takes the same query the user is editing and returns a string, object, or
pair of values depending on the format you ask for. Pick a format below to see the live
output for the query above.

<Formats />

<div class="muted small">

Need a Postgres or MySQL flavour? Pass `{ preset: 'postgresql' }` as the third argument
to `formatQuery`. The full set of presets is sqlite, postgresql, mysql, mssql, oracle,
and ansi.

</div>

</section>

<section id="duckdb">

## Use with DuckDB or any database

When you want to run the query against a real database, ask for the parameterized format.
You get back a SQL string with placeholders and a separate array of values. Hand both to a
prepared statement.

```ts
import { formatQuery } from 'svelte-query-builder';

$effect(() => {
  const { sql, params } = formatQuery(query, 'parameterized');
  void run(sql, params);
});

async function run(where: string, params: unknown[]) {
  const stmt = await connection.prepare(`SELECT * FROM users WHERE ${where}`);
  results = await stmt.query(...params);
}
```

<div class="muted small">

Always use a parameterized format when feeding a real database. Never paste the result of
`formatQuery(q, 'sql')` directly into a query string. The plain SQL output is for display.

</div>

</section>

<section id="controls">

## Custom value editors

The default value editor handles text, numbers, dates, checkboxes, switches, and selects.
When you need something else, write your own Svelte component and pass it through the
`controls` prop. The builder gives it the rule, the current value, and a function to call
when the value changes.

<CustomEditor />

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import { Input } from '$lib/components/ui/input/index.js';
  import type { ValueEditorProps } from 'svelte-query-builder';

  let { value, onChange, disabled }: ValueEditorProps = $props();
</script>

<Input
  type="text"
  {disabled}
  value={value == null ? '' : String(value)}
  oninput={(e) => onChange(e.currentTarget.value.toUpperCase())}
/>
```

Slots you can override include `valueEditor`, `valueSelector`, `fieldSelector`,
`operatorSelector`, `combinatorSelector`, `notToggle`, `shiftActions`, `matchModeEditor`,
`dragHandle`, plus all the action buttons (add rule, add group, remove, clone, lock,
mute). Set a slot to `null` to hide it entirely.

</section>

<section id="snippets">

## Layout slots

When you want to add UI above or below the rule tree without rebuilding anything, use the
`header` and `footer` snippets. They receive the same schema and query the builder is
using internally.

<Snippets />

```svelte
<QueryBuilder bind:query {fields}>
  {#snippet header({ schema })}
    <div>{schema.fields.length} fields available</div>
  {/snippet}
  {#snippet footer({ query })}
    <pre>{formatQuery(query, 'sql')}</pre>
  {/snippet}
</QueryBuilder>
```

</section>

<section id="theming">

## Theming

The default look uses Tailwind v4 CSS variables. You can change the theme three ways, from
broadest to narrowest.

1. Edit the OKLch CSS variables in your project's `app.css`. This affects every
   shadcn-svelte component, not just the builder.
2. Pass `controlClassnames` with the parts you want to override. Each key maps to one
   piece of the UI.
3. Write CSS that targets `data-rqb-element` attributes. Every rendered node has one.
   This works without classes.

Same query, two different visual treatments.

<Theming />

<div class="muted small">

The variant uses only `controlClassnames` and a few scoped CSS rules that target the
`data-rqb-element` attributes. No fork. No custom components.

</div>

</section>

<section id="ic">

## Inline combinators

By default each group has one combinator that joins all of its rules. Set
`independentCombinators` to switch to a flat layout where the user picks AND or OR between
every rule.

<InlineCombinators />

</section>

<section id="lock">

## Lock and mute

Locked rules cannot be edited. They show as dimmed and the form controls become read only.
Muted rules stay in the JSON tree but the format functions skip them, so they do not appear
in your SQL or MongoDB output. The two are independent and can be toggled separately.

<LockMute />

</section>

<section id="validation">

## Validation

Pass a function to `validator`. It receives the current query and returns a map of rule id
to validity. Anything you mark invalid gets the class `queryBuilder-invalid`. You add
styling by mapping that class to whatever you want through `controlClassnames`.

<Validation />

```ts
const validator: QueryValidator = (q) => {
  const map: ValidationMap = {};
  walk(q, (node) => {
    if (node.value === '' || node.value == null) {
      map[node.id] = { valid: false, reasons: ['empty'] };
    }
  });
  return map;
};
```

</section>

<section id="sources">

## Compare to a field instead of a value

Add `valueSources: ['value', 'field']` to a field definition and the builder adds a small
selector that lets the user choose between typing a literal and picking another field. The
first rule below compares two date fields directly.

<ValueSources />

</section>

<section id="match">

## Array match modes

When a field holds a list (a column of phone numbers, tags, or anything similar) you often
want to ask questions like "at least two of them contain 555". Set `matchModes: true` on
the field and the builder shows a small selector with all six modes: all, some, none, at
least, at most, and exactly. The numeric ones get a threshold input.

<Match />

</section>

<section id="between">

## Between and lists

The `between` and `notBetween` operators show two inputs side by side. The `in` and
`notIn` operators take a comma separated list of values. Both work without configuration
and feed the format functions correctly.

<Between />

</section>

<section id="dnd">

## Drag and drop

Set `enableDragAndDrop` to let users reorder rules and move them between groups. Each
group's body becomes a drop zone and shares a type with the others, so rules can move from
one group to another.

<Dnd />

<div class="muted small">

If you prefer, import the wrapper component instead:
`import { QueryBuilderDnD } from 'svelte-query-builder/dnd'`. It is the same builder with
the flag set for you.

</div>

</section>

<section id="async">

## Async option lists

When the list of options for a field comes from a server, use `createAsyncOptionList`. It
exposes status, data, and error as reactive properties, and it cancels in flight requests
automatically when you call `load` again.

```ts
import { createAsyncOptionList } from 'svelte-query-builder';

const list = createAsyncOptionList({
  fetcher: async (input, signal) => {
    const r = await fetch(`/api/options?q=${input}`, { signal });
    return r.json();
  },
});

$effect(() => list.load(currentField));

// list.status — 'idle' | 'loading' | 'success' | 'error'
// list.data   — array of options or null
// list.error  — Error or null
```

</section>

<section id="headless">

## Bring your own UI

The default look depends on shadcn-svelte. If you want to ship without that, swap every
subcomponent through the `controls` prop. The example below uses plain HTML `<select>`,
`<input>`, and `<button>` elements. No shadcn, no bits-ui, no Tailwind utility classes.

<Headless />

For the rare case where you also want to ditch the `QueryBuilder` orchestrator and write
your own root, import from the `/headless` entry point. You get the state factory and
context helpers with no UI components attached.

```ts
import {
  createQueryBuilderState,
  setQueryBuilderContext,
} from 'svelte-query-builder/headless';

const state = createQueryBuilderState({
  getQuery: () => query,
  defaultQuery: { combinator: 'and', rules: [] },
  onQueryChange: (q) => { query = q; },
});

setQueryBuilderContext({
  get state()  { return state; },
  get schema() { return mySchema; },
});
```

</section>

<section id="subpaths">

## Subpath imports

The main entry point exposes everything the typical app needs. For smaller bundles, the
format and parser helpers are also available as separate entry points.

| Path | What it exports |
| --- | --- |
| `svelte-query-builder` | QueryBuilder, every default subcomponent, types, defaults, format and transform helpers. |
| `svelte-query-builder/headless` | Types, defaults, state factory, and context helpers. No UI. |
| `svelte-query-builder/dnd` | QueryBuilderDnD wrapper component. |
| `svelte-query-builder/formatQuery` | Just the formatQuery function. |
| `svelte-query-builder/transformQuery` | Just the transformQuery function. |
| `svelte-query-builder/parseSQL` | Parse a SQL WHERE clause back into a query. |
| `svelte-query-builder/parseMongoDB` | Parse a MongoDB query object. |
| `svelte-query-builder/parseCEL` | Parse a Common Expression Language string. |
| `svelte-query-builder/parseSpEL` | Parse a Spring Expression Language string. |
| `svelte-query-builder/parseJSONata` | Parse a JSONata expression. Needs the optional jsonata package. |
| `svelte-query-builder/parseJsonLogic` | Parse a JSON Logic value. Needs the optional json-logic-js package. |

</section>

<section id="pitfalls">

## Things to watch out for

**Use `$state` for the query variable.**
The builder needs reactive access to the query. Plain variables will not work with
`bind:query`.

**The default SQL output is for display.**
When running queries against a real database, always use `'parameterized'` or
`'parameterized_named'`. The plain `'sql'` format does not escape values for arbitrary
injection paths.

**There is no DuckDB preset.**
Use the `sqlite` preset (closest dialect) or, better, the `parameterized` format which
avoids most dialect specific issues.

**Inline combinator queries have a different shape.**
When you set `independentCombinators`, your default query must use `RuleGroupTypeIC`: no
top level `combinator`, and the `rules` array alternates between rule objects and
combinator strings.

**Cloning a rule inserts it right after the original.**
Calling `state.cloneNode([0])` places the copy at index `1` in the same parent. This
keeps the visual order predictable.

</section>

<footer class="docs-footer">

This library wraps `@react-querybuilder/core`, which does the heavy lifting for format
conversion and parsing. The default look uses shadcn-svelte and bits-ui on top of Tailwind
v4. Both libraries are MIT licensed.

</footer>
